    {
      role: 'user',
      content: `I take the escort slip to the counter. "The cart escort to Reedford — mark that one taken for me. Where's the dyers' yard by the east gate, and is the route safe by day?"`
    },
    {
      role: 'assistant',
      content: `The clerk took the slip back, made a note in the ledger, and stamped it taken with the same brass stamp. "There, it's yours. Nobody else can pin it now." A second slip came across the counter, the seal reading active, and he slid it over beside the first.\n` +
        '\n' +
        `"Dyers' yard — out the east gate, turn left along the wall, follow the smell. Vats and wet wool, you'll know it before you see it. Ask for Mistress Odell." He set the stamp back in its drawer. "Safe by day, mostly. The trouble's been at the ford crossing, evening and dusk, which is exactly when a loaded cart comes late to Reedford. River road's open ground the whole way — no cover for a man on foot to hide in, which cuts both ways. Carts have been robbed, not killed, so far. They take the dye, the drivers keep their teeth."\n` +
        '\n' +
        'He straightened the ledger and looked at Alaric properly for the first time.\n' +
        '\n' +
        `"You're Novice, traveling alone. Odell pays for a guard, not a hero — she'll want to see a plate and a staff and nothing more. First light, remember. She won't wait past it, she loses the day's trade."`
    },
    {
      role: 'user',
      content: "I leave the Guild and find the dyers' yard by the east gate to introduce myself to Mistress Odell before tomorrow's muster, then look for a cheap room for the night nearby."
    },
    {
      role: 'assistant',
      content: "The east gate spilled him out past the slaughter stalls and the tannery runs, and the clerk had not lied about the smell. It reached him before the wall's shadow ended — sharp urine, wet wool, and under it the plant bitterness of the dye itself. The yard lay along the inside of the wall where the road bent: a cobbled court half-filled with sweating vats, wool hung on lines like drowned things, and boys with poles turning the cloth in the heat.\n" +
        '\n' +
        'A broad woman in a leather apron streaked red to the elbow stood over the largest vat, stirring with a stick longer than she was. She saw the staff and the plate before she saw his face.\n' +
        '\n' +
        `"Guild?" She didn't stop stirring. "Odell. You'll be the guard for tomorrow, then. Show me."\n` +
        '\n' +
        'He turned the plate toward her and she leaned over the vat to read it, lips moving.\n' +
        '\n' +
        `"Novice. Mage." She straightened and looked him up and down, taking in the robes, the thin arms, the road dust. "Huh. Well, waylayers at a ford want stopping, not wrestling, so maybe that's sense." She pointed the stick at a low shed by the gate. "Cart rolls at first light, from that door there. Driver's Tam — old, careful, knows the road better than I do. You ride on the bench or walk alongside, your choice. Two vats of madder on the bed, worth more than the horse and the cart together, so if trouble comes, it comes for the vats."\n` +
        '\n' +
        'She went back to her stirring, then paused.\n' +
        '\n' +
        `"You eat?" A jerk of the chin toward a bench by the wall where a girl was cutting bread. "Sit if you like. I'll want a word about the road before you go."\n` +
        '\n' +
        'The room came cheap that evening, two streets back from the east gate at a sign of a leaning bell: a cot under a sloped roof, a straw tick that had known better years, and a landlady who counted his copper twice before naming three copper for the night, water and a boot of small beer by the common fire included. Downstairs, carters argued over dice and the smell of onions came up through the floorboards. First light was a long way off yet, and the road to Reedford with it.'
    },
    {
      role: 'user',
      content: 'I take the boot of small beer by the fire and ask the carters whether any of them have run the Reedford road lately, and what the waylayers at the ford are like.'
    },
    {
      role: 'assistant',
      content: "The beer was thin and slightly sour, but it was wet, and the fire took the evening chill off his back. The dice game had broken up into grumbling and a dispute over two coppers that nobody would admit to losing, and the nearest carter — a heavyset man with a knuckle scarred shiny — gave Alaric's question half an ear while he counted his winnings.\n" +
        '\n' +
        `"Reedford road? Ran it a fortnight back, hauling hides. Daytime, mind, and we came through the ford by noon." He swept the coppers into a fist. "You're asking about the dye robberies, though. Everyone's asking."\n` +
        '\n' +
        `"Ford crossing," said an older man by the fire, not looking up from mending a whip lash. "Evening and dusk, that's when they've hit. Four times since the spring, always dye carts, always the same stretch. That's not luck, that's knowing what to watch for."\n` +
        '\n' +
        `"Could be the dyers' own schedule's too regular," the first carter said. "Could be somebody in Alderwatch tells them what's rolling and when." He shrugged at the looks this got. "Well, how else do they always pick the loaded ones? Nobody robs an empty cart twice."\n` +
        '\n' +
        `The older man bit his thread and tied it off. "Nobody's seen them proper. Drivers say it happens fast — men out of the willow scrub on the south bank, where the trees come down close to the water, before the cart's halfway across. Four or five of them, bows half-drawn, and a knife-man who does the talking. Polite, by all accounts. 'Unload the vats and keep your teeth.'" He snorted. "They've never hurt a driver yet, but they've never let one keep so much as a rag of the cargo either. And they knew where the guards sat, first job. Told the guard to keep his seat and keep his nose out, and he did, because what's one sword against five men in the trees?"\n` +
        '\n' +
        "He tested the mended lash against his knee, then glanced up at Alaric's staff.\n" +
        '\n' +
        `"You're taking Odell's cart, then. Well — cross the ford before the sun's low and you'll likely never meet them. They're not brave, they're patient. There's a difference."\n` +
        '\n' +
        `The first carter gathered his winnings and stood, stretching. "Or they're patient till the day they're not. Tam's a careful man, though. He'll want to push through early. Listen to him."`
    },
    {
      role: 'user',
      content: "I finish the beer and turn in for the night, sleeping until first light so I'm at the dyers' yard shed in time for the muster with Tam."
    },
    {
      role: 'system',
      content: '[AVERETH ENGINE — authoritative game state, turn 23. Numbers, rolls, positions and knowledge below are binding; narrate, never recalculate.]\n' +
        'Day 2, 06:00 (early morning) | Leaning Bell inn, Valedorn Crown | mode: story\n' +
        'Setting: Western-fantasy medieval material culture with mana/high magic; letters and messengers for distance; no modern technology.\n' +
        '\n' +
        'Alaric — Level 1 Mage (three-class rules), Power Rank F, Guild Novice | HP 80/80 (unhurt) MP 72/72 STA 100/100\n' +
        "STR 5 VIT 5 AGI 5 INT 6 PER 5 WIL 6 | Equipped: Simple Traveler's Clothes, Starter Arcane Staff, Starter Light Robes\n" +
        '\n' +
