    {
      role: 'assistant',
      content: "The beer was thin and slightly sour, but it was wet, and the fire took the evening chill off his back. The dice game had broken up into grumbling and a dispute over two coppers that nobody would admit to losing, and the nearest carter — a heavyset man with a knuckle scarred shiny — gave Alaric's question half an ear while he counted his winnings.\n" +
        '\n' +
        `"Reedford road? Ran it a fortnight back, hauling hides. Daytime, mind, and we came through the ford by noon." He swept the coppers into a fist. "You're asking about the dye robberies, though. Everyone's asking."\n` +
        '\n' +
        `"Ford crossing," said an older man by the fire, not looking up from mending a whip lash. "Evening and dusk, that's when they've hit. Four times since the spring, always dye carts, always the same stretch. That's not luck, that's knowing what to watch for."\n` +
        '\n' +
        `"Could be the dyers' own schedule's too regular," the first carter said. "Could be somebody in Alderwatch tells them what's rolling and when." He shrugged at the looks this got. "Well, how else do they always pick the loaded ones? Nobody robs an empty cart twice."\n` +
        '\n' +
        `The older man bit his thread and tied it off. "Nobody's seen them proper. Drivers say it happens fast — men out of the willow scrub on the south bank, where the trees come down close to the water, before the cart's halfway across. Four or five of them, bows half-drawn, and a knife-man who does the talking. Polite, by all accounts. 'Unload the vats and keep your teeth.'" He snorted. "They've never hurt a driver yet, but they've never let one keep so much as a rag of the cargo either. And they knew where the guards sat, first job. Told the guard to keep his seat and keep his nose out, and he did, because what's one sword against five men in the trees?"\n` +
        '\n' +
        "He tested the mended lash against his knee, then glanced up at Alaric's staff.\n" +
        '\n' +
        `"You're taking Odell's cart, then. Well — cross the ford before the sun's low and you'll likely never meet them. They're not brave, they're patient. There's a difference."\n` +
        '\n' +
        `The first carter gathered his winnings and stood, stretching. "Or they're patient till the day they're not. Tam's a careful man, though. He'll want to push through early. Listen to him."`
